{\plain {\fs20 \b Thus, we have observed
 that the zeros have occurred in a little
 more complicated manner. There is a quadruplet
 set which are "closed" under complex conjugation
 and inversion. Notice that there is also
 a real pair, not equal to +/- 1, which 
are their own conjugates and are reciprocals:
 again, the pair is }{\fs20 \b \fs20 \b 
"closed" under complex conjugation and inversion.
 Finally, the zero at z=1 is its own complex
 conjugate and its own reciprocal: so it
 is a single-member set that is }{\fs20 
\b \fs20 \b "closed" under complex conjugation
 and inversion. The same statment applies
 to the zero at z+-1.}}
}
